1
00:00:05,078 --> 00:00:10,160
In the room where the dressing-table stood, and where the wax-candles burnt on the wall,

2
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:16,160
I found Miss Havisham and Estella; Miss Havisham seated on a settee near the fire,

3
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:20,758
and Estella on a cushion at her feet. Estella was knitting,

4
00:00:20,758 --> 00:00:25,597
and Miss Havisham was looking on. They both raised their eyes as I went in,

5
00:00:25,597 --> 00:00:31,038
and both saw an alteration in me. I derived that, from the look they interchanged.

6
00:00:31,641 --> 00:00:36,199
“And what wind,” said Miss Havisham, “blows you here, Pip?”

7
00:00:36,199 --> 00:00:41,758
Though she looked steadily at me, I saw that she was rather confused. Estella,

8
00:00:41,758 --> 00:00:46,480
pausing a moment in her knitting with her eyes upon me, and then going on,

9
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:51,160
I fancied that I read in the action of her fingers, as plainly as if she had told me in

10
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:57,718
the dumb alphabet, that she perceived I had discovered my real benefactor.

11
00:00:57,718 --> 00:01:01,480
“Miss Havisham,” said I, “I went to Richmond yesterday,

12
00:01:01,480 --> 00:01:07,796
to speak to Estella; and finding that some wind had blown her here, I followed.”

13
00:01:07,796 --> 00:01:12,641
Miss Havisham motioning to me for the third or fourth time to sit down,

14
00:01:12,641 --> 00:01:17,398
I took the chair by the dressing-table, which I had often seen her occupy. With

15
00:01:17,398 --> 00:01:24,078
all that ruin at my feet and about me, it seemed a natural place for me, that day.

16
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:26,641
“What I had to say to Estella, Miss Havisham,

17
00:01:26,641 --> 00:01:31,957
I will say before you, presently—in a few moments. It will not surprise you,

18
00:01:31,957 --> 00:01:37,718
it will not displease you. I am as unhappy as you can ever have meant me to be.”

19
00:01:37,718 --> 00:01:43,116
Miss Havisham continued to look steadily at me. I could see in the action of Estella’s

20
00:01:43,116 --> 00:01:49,718
fingers as they worked that she attended to what I said; but she did not look up.

21
00:01:49,718 --> 00:01:55,319
“I have found out who my patron is. It is not a fortunate discovery,

22
00:01:55,319 --> 00:02:01,398
and is not likely ever to enrich me in reputation, station, fortune, anything.

23
00:02:01,398 --> 00:02:08,398
There are reasons why I must say no more of that. It is not my secret, but another’s.”

24
00:02:08,398 --> 00:02:12,838
As I was silent for a while, looking at Estella and considering how to go on,

25
00:02:12,839 --> 00:02:20,358
Miss Havisham repeated, “It is not your secret, but another’s. Well?”

26
00:02:20,358 --> 00:02:25,078
“When you first caused me to be brought here, Miss Havisham, when I belonged to the village over

27
00:02:25,078 --> 00:02:31,078
yonder, that I wish I had never left, I suppose I did really come here, as any other chance

28
00:02:31,078 --> 00:02:38,038
boy might have come,—as a kind of servant, to gratify a want or a whim, and to be paid for it?”

29
00:02:38,038 --> 00:02:43,398
“Ay, Pip,” replied Miss Havisham, steadily nodding her head; “you did.”

30
00:02:43,398 --> 00:02:45,718
“And that Mr. Jaggers—”

31
00:02:45,718 --> 00:02:51,957
“Mr. Jaggers,” said Miss Havisham, taking me up in a firm tone, “had nothing to do with it,

32
00:02:51,957 --> 00:02:56,237
and knew nothing of it. His being my lawyer, and his being the lawyer of

33
00:02:56,237 --> 00:03:02,160
your patron is a coincidence. He holds the same relation towards numbers of people,

34
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:10,639
and it might easily arise. Be that as it may, it did arise, and was not brought about by any one.”

35
00:03:10,641 --> 00:03:16,000
Any one might have seen in her haggard face that there was no suppression or evasion so far.

36
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,237
“But when I fell into the mistake I have so long remained in, at least you led me on?” said I.

37
00:03:22,237 --> 00:03:28,000
“Yes,” she returned, again nodding steadily, “I let you go on.”

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:29,957
“Was that kind?”

39
00:03:29,957 --> 00:03:35,398
“Who am I,” cried Miss Havisham, striking her stick upon the floor and flashing into wrath

40
00:03:35,398 --> 00:03:40,078
so suddenly that Estella glanced up at her in surprise,—“who am I,

41
00:03:40,078 --> 00:03:43,436
for God’s sake, that I should be kind?”

42
00:03:43,438 --> 00:03:49,277
It was a weak complaint to have made, and I had not meant to make it. I told her so,

43
00:03:49,277 --> 00:03:52,199
as she sat brooding after this outburst.

44
00:03:52,199 --> 00:03:55,879
“Well, well, well!” she said. “What else?”

45
00:03:55,879 --> 00:04:02,160
“I was liberally paid for my old attendance here,” I said, to soothe her, “in being apprenticed,

46
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:07,917
and I have asked these questions only for my own information. What follows has another

47
00:04:07,917 --> 00:04:14,718
(and I hope more disinterested) purpose. In humouring my mistake, Miss Havisham,

48
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:20,398
you punished—practised on—perhaps you will supply whatever term expresses your intention,

49
00:04:20,398 --> 00:04:24,398
without offence—your self-seeking relations?”

50
00:04:24,398 --> 00:04:31,116
“I did. Why, they would have it so! So would you. What has been my history,

51
00:04:31,116 --> 00:04:34,038
that I should be at the pains of entreating either them or you

52
00:04:34,038 --> 00:04:40,116
not to have it so! You made your own snares. I never made them.”

53
00:04:40,116 --> 00:04:43,319
Waiting until she was quiet again,—for this, too,

54
00:04:43,319 --> 00:04:48,398
flashed out of her in a wild and sudden way,—I went on.

55
00:04:48,398 --> 00:04:52,237
“I have been thrown among one family of your relations, Miss Havisham,

56
00:04:52,237 --> 00:04:57,957
and have been constantly among them since I went to London. I know them to have been as

57
00:04:57,957 --> 00:05:04,436
honestly under my delusion as I myself. And I should be false and base if I did not tell you,

58
00:05:04,438 --> 00:05:10,559
whether it is acceptable to you or no, and whether you are inclined to give credence to it or no,

59
00:05:10,559 --> 00:05:15,718
that you deeply wrong both Mr. Matthew Pocket and his son Herbert, if you suppose them to be

60
00:05:15,718 --> 00:05:22,116
otherwise than generous, upright, open, and incapable of anything designing or mean.”

61
00:05:22,116 --> 00:05:25,000
“They are your friends,” said Miss Havisham.

62
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,796
“They made themselves my friends,” said I, “when they supposed me to have superseded them;

63
00:05:30,796 --> 00:05:36,319
and when Sarah Pocket, Miss Georgiana, and Mistress Camilla were not my friends, I think.”

64
00:05:36,319 --> 00:05:39,436
This contrasting of them with the rest seemed, I was glad to see,

65
00:05:39,438 --> 00:05:46,319
to do them good with her. She looked at me keenly for a little while, and then said quietly,—

66
00:05:46,319 --> 00:05:52,557
“What do you want for them?” “Only,” said I, “that you would not confound them

67
00:05:52,559 --> 00:05:58,839
with the others. They may be of the same blood, but, believe me, they are not of the same nature.”

68
00:05:58,839 --> 00:06:01,879
Still looking at me keenly, Miss Havisham repeated,—

69
00:06:01,879 --> 00:06:03,718
“What do you want for them?”

70
00:06:03,718 --> 00:06:08,559
“I am not so cunning, you see,” I said, in answer, conscious that I reddened a little,

71
00:06:08,559 --> 00:06:13,796
“as that I could hide from you, even if I desired, that I do want something. Miss

72
00:06:13,796 --> 00:06:19,597
Havisham, if you would spare the money to do my friend Herbert a lasting service in life,

73
00:06:19,597 --> 00:06:25,839
but which from the nature of the case must be done without his knowledge, I could show you how.”

74
00:06:25,839 --> 00:06:29,160
“Why must it be done without his knowledge?” she asked,

75
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:35,038
settling her hands upon her stick, that she might regard me the more attentively.

76
00:06:35,038 --> 00:06:41,398
“Because,” said I, “I began the service myself, more than two years ago, without his knowledge,

77
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:47,597
and I don’t want to be betrayed. Why I fail in my ability to finish it,

78
00:06:47,597 --> 00:06:54,680
I cannot explain. It is a part of the secret which is another person’s and not mine.”

79
00:06:54,680 --> 00:07:00,199
She gradually withdrew her eyes from me, and turned them on the fire. After watching it

80
00:07:00,199 --> 00:07:06,038
for what appeared in the silence and by the light of the slowly wasting candles to be a long time,

81
00:07:06,038 --> 00:07:11,917
she was roused by the collapse of some of the red coals, and looked towards me again—at first,

82
00:07:11,917 --> 00:07:17,796
vacantly—then, with a gradually concentrating attention. All

83
00:07:17,796 --> 00:07:23,237
this time Estella knitted on. When Miss Havisham had fixed her attention on me,

84
00:07:23,237 --> 00:07:27,358
she said, speaking as if there had been no lapse in our dialogue,—

85
00:07:27,358 --> 00:07:29,358
“What else?”

86
00:07:29,358 --> 00:07:35,639
“Estella,” said I, turning to her now, and trying to command my trembling voice,

87
00:07:35,641 --> 00:07:41,559
“you know I love you. You know that I have loved you long and dearly.”

88
00:07:41,559 --> 00:07:46,839
She raised her eyes to my face, on being thus addressed, and her fingers plied their work,

89
00:07:46,839 --> 00:07:50,519
and she looked at me with an unmoved countenance.

90
00:07:50,519 --> 00:07:56,480
I saw that Miss Havisham glanced from me to her, and from her to me.

91
00:07:56,480 --> 00:08:01,718
“I should have said this sooner, but for my long mistake. It induced me to hope

92
00:08:01,718 --> 00:08:06,796
that Miss Havisham meant us for one another. While I thought you could not help yourself,

93
00:08:06,796 --> 00:08:13,000
as it were, I refrained from saying it. But I must say it now.”

94
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:20,358
Preserving her unmoved countenance, and with her fingers still going, Estella shook her head.

95
00:08:20,358 --> 00:08:26,358
“I know,” said I, in answer to that action,—“I know. I have no hope that I shall ever call

96
00:08:26,358 --> 00:08:33,160
you mine, Estella. I am ignorant what may become of me very soon, how poor I may be,

97
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:43,116
or where I may go. Still, I love you. I have loved you ever since I first saw you in this house.”

98
00:08:43,116 --> 00:08:49,116
Looking at me perfectly unmoved and with her fingers busy, she shook her head again.

99
00:08:49,116 --> 00:08:54,078
“It would have been cruel in Miss Havisham, horribly cruel, to practise on the susceptibility

100
00:08:54,078 --> 00:09:00,641
of a poor boy, and to torture me through all these years with a vain hope and an idle pursuit,

101
00:09:00,641 --> 00:09:06,038
if she had reflected on the gravity of what she did. But I think she did not.

102
00:09:06,038 --> 00:09:13,116
I think that, in the endurance of her own trial, she forgot mine, Estella.”

103
00:09:13,116 --> 00:09:16,758
I saw Miss Havisham put her hand to her heart and hold it there,

104
00:09:16,758 --> 00:09:21,237
as she sat looking by turns at Estella and at me.

105
00:09:21,237 --> 00:09:27,917
“It seems,” said Estella, very calmly, “that there are sentiments, fancies,—I don’t know how

106
00:09:27,917 --> 00:09:36,237
to call them,—which I am not able to comprehend. When you say you love me, I know what you mean,

107
00:09:36,237 --> 00:09:43,718
as a form of words; but nothing more. You address nothing in my breast, you touch nothing there.

108
00:09:43,718 --> 00:09:50,917
I don’t care for what you say at all. I have tried to warn you of this; now, have I not?”

109
00:09:50,917 --> 00:09:54,078
I said in a miserable manner, “Yes.”

110
00:09:54,078 --> 00:10:00,000
“Yes. But you would not be warned, for you thought I did not mean it. Now, did you not think so?”

111
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,078
“I thought and hoped you could not mean it. You,

112
00:10:03,078 --> 00:10:08,078
so young, untried, and beautiful, Estella! Surely it is not in Nature.”

113
00:10:08,078 --> 00:10:14,358
“It is in my nature,” she returned. And then she added, with a stress upon the words,

114
00:10:14,358 --> 00:10:18,796
“It is in the nature formed within me. I make a great difference between

115
00:10:18,796 --> 00:10:24,078
you and all other people when I say so much. I can do no more.”

116
00:10:24,078 --> 00:10:30,879
“Is it not true,” said I, “that Bentley Drummle is in town here, and pursuing you?”

117
00:10:30,879 --> 00:10:36,199
“It is quite true,” she replied, referring to him with the indifference of utter contempt.

118
00:10:36,199 --> 00:10:42,237
“That you encourage him, and ride out with him, and that he dines with you this very day?”

119
00:10:42,237 --> 00:10:49,319
She seemed a little surprised that I should know it, but again replied, “Quite true.”

120
00:10:49,319 --> 00:10:51,957
“You cannot love him, Estella!”

121
00:10:51,957 --> 00:10:56,597
Her fingers stopped for the first time, as she retorted rather angrily,

122
00:10:56,597 --> 00:11:03,199
“What have I told you? Do you still think, in spite of it, that I do not mean what I say?”

123
00:11:03,199 --> 00:11:06,398
“You would never marry him, Estella?”

124
00:11:06,398 --> 00:11:10,277
She looked towards Miss Havisham, and considered for a moment with her work

125
00:11:10,277 --> 00:11:18,160
in her hands. Then she said, “Why not tell you the truth? I am going to be married to him.”

126
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:23,839
I dropped my face into my hands, but was able to control myself better than I could have expected,

127
00:11:23,839 --> 00:11:29,680
considering what agony it gave me to hear her say those words. When I raised my face again,

128
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:34,078
there was such a ghastly look upon Miss Havisham’s, that it impressed me,

129
00:11:34,078 --> 00:11:41,519
even in my passionate hurry and grief. “Estella, dearest Estella, do not let Miss

130
00:11:41,519 --> 00:11:47,957
Havisham lead you into this fatal step. Put me aside for ever,—you have done so, I well

131
00:11:47,957 --> 00:11:54,718
know,—but bestow yourself on some worthier person than Drummle. Miss Havisham gives you to him,

132
00:11:54,718 --> 00:11:59,957
as the greatest slight and injury that could be done to the many far better men who admire you,

133
00:11:59,957 --> 00:12:06,398
and to the few who truly love you. Among those few there may be one who loves you even as dearly,

134
00:12:06,398 --> 00:12:13,519
though he has not loved you as long, as I. Take him, and I can bear it better, for your sake!”

135
00:12:13,519 --> 00:12:18,917
My earnestness awoke a wonder in her that seemed as if it would have been touched with compassion,

136
00:12:18,917 --> 00:12:23,398
if she could have rendered me at all intelligible to her own mind.

137
00:12:23,398 --> 00:12:26,957
“I am going,” she said again, in a gentler voice,

138
00:12:26,957 --> 00:12:32,641
“to be married to him. The preparations for my marriage are making, and I shall be married

139
00:12:32,641 --> 00:12:40,796
soon. Why do you injuriously introduce the name of my mother by adoption? It is my own act.”

140
00:12:40,796 --> 00:12:46,718
“Your own act, Estella, to fling yourself away upon a brute?”

141
00:12:46,718 --> 00:12:52,116
“On whom should I fling myself away?” she retorted, with a smile. “Should I

142
00:12:52,116 --> 00:12:57,718
fling myself away upon the man who would the soonest feel (if people do feel such things)

143
00:12:57,718 --> 00:13:04,160
that I took nothing to him? There! It is done. I shall do well enough, and so will my husband. As

144
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:08,597
to leading me into what you call this fatal step, Miss Havisham would have had me wait,

145
00:13:08,597 --> 00:13:14,480
and not marry yet; but I am tired of the life I have led, which has very few charms for me,

146
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:20,718
and I am willing enough to change it. Say no more. We shall never understand each other.”

147
00:13:20,718 --> 00:13:25,237
“Such a mean brute, such a stupid brute!” I urged, in despair.

148
00:13:25,237 --> 00:13:28,237
“Don’t be afraid of my being a blessing to him,” said Estella;

149
00:13:28,237 --> 00:13:35,078
“I shall not be that. Come! Here is my hand. Do we part on this, you visionary boy—or man?”

150
00:13:35,078 --> 00:13:39,398
“O Estella!” I answered, as my bitter tears fell fast on her hand,

151
00:13:39,398 --> 00:13:43,398
do what I would to restrain them; “even if I remained in England and

152
00:13:43,398 --> 00:13:48,160
could hold my head up with the rest, how could I see you Drummle’s wife?”

153
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:53,078
“Nonsense,” she returned,—“nonsense. This will pass in no time.”

154
00:13:53,078 --> 00:13:54,839
“Never, Estella!”

155
00:13:54,839 --> 00:13:57,718
“You will get me out of your thoughts in a week.”

156
00:13:57,718 --> 00:14:04,480
“Out of my thoughts! You are part of my existence, part of myself. You have been in every line I

157
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:12,398
have ever read since I first came here, the rough common boy whose poor heart you wounded even then.

158
00:14:12,398 --> 00:14:19,319
You have been in every prospect I have ever seen since,—on the river, on the sails of the ships,

159
00:14:19,319 --> 00:14:24,879
on the marshes, in the clouds, in the light, in the darkness, in the wind, in the woods,

160
00:14:24,879 --> 00:14:30,438
in the sea, in the streets. You have been the embodiment of every graceful fancy

161
00:14:30,438 --> 00:14:35,078
that my mind has ever become acquainted with. The stones of which the strongest

162
00:14:35,078 --> 00:14:40,879
London buildings are made are not more real, or more impossible to be displaced by your hands,

163
00:14:40,879 --> 00:14:48,597
than your presence and influence have been to me, there and everywhere, and will be. Estella,

164
00:14:48,597 --> 00:14:54,641
to the last hour of my life, you cannot choose but remain part of my character, part of the

165
00:14:54,641 --> 00:15:01,160
little good in me, part of the evil. But, in this separation, I associate you only with the good;

166
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:07,597
and I will faithfully hold you to that always, for you must have done me far more good than harm,

167
00:15:07,597 --> 00:15:15,038
let me feel now what sharp distress I may. O God bless you, God forgive you!”

168
00:15:15,038 --> 00:15:19,319
In what ecstasy of unhappiness I got these broken words out of myself,

169
00:15:19,319 --> 00:15:23,917
I don’t know. The rhapsody welled up within me, like blood from an inward wound,

170
00:15:23,917 --> 00:15:30,038
and gushed out. I held her hand to my lips some lingering moments, and so I left her.

171
00:15:31,160 --> 00:15:36,078
But ever afterwards, I remembered,—and soon afterwards with stronger reason,—that while

172
00:15:36,078 --> 00:15:41,597
Estella looked at me merely with incredulous wonder, the spectral figure of Miss Havisham,

173
00:15:41,597 --> 00:15:49,038
her hand still covering her heart, seemed all resolved into a ghastly stare of pity and remorse.

174
00:15:49,038 --> 00:15:57,839
All done, all gone! So much was done and gone, that when I went out at the gate,

175
00:15:57,839 --> 00:16:03,038
the light of the day seemed of a darker colour than when I went in. For a while,

176
00:16:03,038 --> 00:16:09,480
I hid myself among some lanes and by-paths, and then struck off to walk all the way to London.

177
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:14,358
For, I had by that time come to myself so far as to consider that I could not go back to the

178
00:16:14,358 --> 00:16:20,038
inn and see Drummle there; that I could not bear to sit upon the coach and be spoken to;

179
00:16:20,038 --> 00:16:24,758
that I could do nothing half so good for myself as tire myself out.

180
00:16:24,758 --> 00:16:29,199
It was past midnight when I crossed London Bridge. Pursuing the narrow

181
00:16:29,199 --> 00:16:32,879
intricacies of the streets which at that time tended westward near the Middlesex

182
00:16:32,879 --> 00:16:37,480
shore of the river, my readiest access to the Temple was close by the river-side,

183
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:43,398
through Whitefriars. I was not expected till to-morrow; but I had my keys, and,

184
00:16:43,398 --> 00:16:50,000
if Herbert were gone to bed, could get to bed myself without disturbing him.

185
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:55,038
As it seldom happened that I came in at that Whitefriars gate after the Temple was closed,

186
00:16:55,038 --> 00:17:00,116
and as I was very muddy and weary, I did not take it ill that the night-porter examined

187
00:17:00,116 --> 00:17:06,116
me with much attention as he held the gate a little way open for me to pass in. To help his

188
00:17:06,116 --> 00:17:11,160
memory I mentioned my name. “I was not quite sure, sir,

189
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:15,319
but I thought so. Here’s a note, sir. The messenger that brought it,

190
00:17:15,319 --> 00:17:19,237
said would you be so good as read it by my lantern?”

191
00:17:19,237 --> 00:17:24,639
Much surprised by the request, I took the note. It was directed to Philip Pip, Esquire,

192
00:17:24,641 --> 00:17:30,796
and on the top of the superscription were the words, “Please Read This, Here.” I opened it,

193
00:17:30,796 --> 00:17:35,480
the watchman holding up his light, and read inside, in Wemmick’s writing,—

194
00:17:35,480 --> 00:17:37,991
“Don’t Go Home.” Chapter 45.
